  <subsection xml:id="cmse381-lec27-svm-worksheet-still-have-time">
    <title>Still have time?</title>
    <p>Download the NIST data set from here: <url href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/optical+recognition+of+handwritten+digits">https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/optical+recognition+of+handwritten+digits</url></p>
    <p>You just need two files for now, the training set <c>optdigits.tra</c> and the testing set <c>optdigits.tes</c>. The following commands will pull the data from the remote server. Optionally, you can download the files directly to your computer.</p>
    <program language="python">
      X_train = pd.read_csv('https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/machine-learning-databases/optdigits/optdigits.tra', header=None)
      X_train.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      X_test = pd.read_csv('https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/machine-learning-databases/optdigits/optdigits.tes', header=None)
      X_test.head()
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      # X_train = pd.read_csv('optdigits.tra', header=None) #&lt;-- put this line back in if you saved the data locally
      y_train = X_train[64]
      X_train = X_train.drop(X_train.columns[64], axis=1)

      # X_test = pd.read_csv('optdigits.tes', header=None) #&lt;-- put this line back in if you saved the data locally
      y_test = X_test[64]
      X_test = X_test.drop(X_test.columns[64], axis=1)
    </program>
    <program language="python">
      print(X_train.shape)
      print(X_test.shape)
    </program>
    <p>This data set consists of 8x8 images of handwritten digits. The following command will draw each data point for you. Mess around with the value of <m>i</m> below to see other examples</p>
    <program language="python">
      i = 13
      plt.imshow(X_train.values[i].reshape(8,8), cmap="gray") 
      plt.show()
      print(f'Data point {i} is labeled as {y_train[i]}')
    </program>
    <!-- horizontal rule in source notebook -->
    <exercises>
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercisegroup>
        <title>Practice</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>Complete the following exercises.</p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Build a classifier to predict the correct digit for a given handwritten digit. As you do this, answer the following questions:</p>
              <p><ul>
                <li>
                  <p>What choice of kernel does best?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>What are the optimal choices of parameters for the SVC?</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>How well does your classifier do? Don't forget that quality measures should always use testing data.</p>
                </li>
              </ul></p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Tune candidate kernels and their relevant parameters using cross-validation on the provided training set only.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>After choosing the best configuration, evaluate it exactly once on the separate testing set with accuracy and a confusion matrix.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> A perfect training confusion matrix does not answer how well the classifier generalizes; parameter selection must not use the test labels.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>A linear SVC is a strong baseline for these 64 pixel features. In the stored run it fits the training set perfectly and obtains about 96.1% test accuracy. A more extensive answer may compare linear, RBF, and polynomial kernels by cross-validation, then report only the chosen model's held-out result.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, ConfusionMatrixDisplay

                  parameter_grid = [
                      {'kernel': ['linear'], 'C': [0.1, 1, 10]},
                      {'kernel': ['rbf'], 'C': [1, 10], 'gamma': ['scale', 0.001, 0.01]}
                  ]
                  search = GridSearchCV(SVC(), parameter_grid, cv=5, scoring='accuracy', n_jobs=-1)
                  search.fit(X_train, y_train)
                  best_digit_svc = search.best_estimator_
                  test_predictions = best_digit_svc.predict(X_test)
                  print('best parameters:', search.best_params_)
                  print('test accuracy:', accuracy_score(y_test, test_predictions))
                  ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions(y_test, test_predictions)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p><ul>
                <li>
                  <p>How well does your above classifier do? Don't forget that quality measures should always use testing data.</p>
                </li>
              </ul></p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>Predict labels for <c>X_test</c>, then compute the fraction matching <c>y_test</c>.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p>Use a confusion matrix to see which individual digits are most often confused.</p>
            </hint>
            <hint>
              <p><alert>Common error:</alert> Keep the conventional confusion-matrix orientation—true labels first and predicted labels second—when interpreting rows and columns.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
              <p>The stored linear-SVC run correctly classifies 1727 of 1797 test images, for approximately 96.1% accuracy. The confusion matrix reveals that errors are concentrated among visually similar digits rather than being evenly distributed.</p>
              <program language="python">
                <code>
                  from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, ConfusionMatrixDisplay
                  test_predictions = best_digit_svc.predict(X_test) if 'best_digit_svc' in globals() else svc.predict(X_test)
                  print('test accuracy:', accuracy_score(y_test, test_predictions))
                  ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_predictions(y_test, test_predictions)
                </code>
              </program>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercisegroup>
    </exercises>
